messages:
alice_cust: Hey team — hitting intermittent streaming disconnects with your SSE endpoint. Client is an internal Node service using HTTP/2. Behavior: we receive a few events, then the stream ends abruptly (no final event, no 200 body finished), and curl shows an HTTP/2 RST_STREAM. If we force HTTP/1.1 it streams fine. Repro steps below. Can someone triage?

amanda: sorry you hit this — we'll take it. quick q: are you going through any proxies/load balancers (GCP/ALB/Envoy) or a gRPC gateway? also can you paste the minimal curl/node repro?

alice_cust: yes it's behind our API gateway (gRPC-gateway -> envoy -> internal cluster). repro:
```
# http2 (broken for us)
curl -v --http2 -N -H "Accept: text/event-stream" \
  "https://api.redwood.example/v1/stream?model=redwood-small&prompt=hi"\n
# http1.1 (works)
curl -v --http1.1 -N -H "Accept: text/event-stream" \
  "https://api.redwood.example/v1/stream?model=redwood-small&prompt=hi"
```
Observed: http2 shows: "[h2] recv RST_STREAM (error_code=2)" and client gets first 2-3 events then disconnect. http1.1 keeps the stream open and we get continuous events.

kevin: thanks, this smells like proxy buffering or improper transfer semantics crossing HTTP/2 <-> HTTP/1.1 translation. Important facts: SSE (text/event-stream) relies on chunked-like flushing. HTTP/2 doesn't use chunked transfer-encoding; it uses DATA frames, so gateways must translate properly. Some gRPC gateways or envoy configurations will buffer until they see end-of-stream and then translate into HTTP/1.1 chunked body for clients — which breaks streaming semantics for h2 clients. Also RST_STREAM indicates the upstream closed the stream or a proxy killed it. Do you have server-side request_id or connection logs?

alice_cust: attaching server logs (scrubbed):
```
[2024-05-05T12:15:01Z] req=abc123 conn=conn-42 send-event: event_id=1
[2024-05-05T12:15:01Z] req=abc123 conn=conn-42 send-event: event_id=2
[2024-05-05T12:15:04Z] req=abc123 conn=conn-42 ERROR: write failed: broken pipe
[2024-05-05T12:15:04Z] req=abc123 conn=conn-42 conn-close: upstream RST_STREAM
```
So upstream sees broken pipe at same time client gets RST.

owen: adding details from runtime side — a few things to check: 
- If any intermediary terminates HTTP/2 and speaks HTTP/1.1 to the upstream, it must convert streaming to chunked and flush periodically. Some gateways buffer full response.
- Nginx default proxy buffering will break SSE. If you use nginx as a reverse proxy, ensure `proxy_buffering off; proxy_request_buffering off;` for that location.
- Envoy has filters that may buffer. In Envoy, ensure you haven't enabled the ``buffer_filter`` on that route; check `use_remote_address`, `stream_idle_timeout`, and per-route `max_stream_duration`.
- gRPC Gateway / translation layers: if they convert to gRPC, they may expect unary responses. gRPC doesn't use SSE; a mismatch will cause abrupt termination.

amanda: actionable next steps for us to triage: (1) try direct-to-runtime bypassing gateway (2) capture h2 frame level trace (nghttp2 or curl -v) (3) confirm whether the gateway is downgrading to http/1.1 to upstream.

kevin: here's a minimal Node http2 SSE server you can run locally to validate h2 behavior (works w/ direct h2 client):
```
// node-http2-sse.js
const http2 = require('http2');
const server = http2.createSecureServer({/* certs for local testing */});
server.on('stream', (stream, headers) => {
  stream.respond({ ':status': 200, 'content-type': 'text/event-stream' });
  let i = 0;
  const iv = setInterval(() => {
    i++;
    stream.write(`data: event ${i}\n\n`);
    if (i === 10) {
      clearInterval(iv);
      stream.end();
    }
  }, 500);
});
server.listen(8443);
```
Test with: `curl --http2 -v -N https://localhost:8443/` should get a steady sequence of events. If your gateway drops this but direct to the server works, gateway is the culprit.

owen: on the Redwood side — our streaming runtime sends framing as DATA frames and flushes on each event. We don't send `transfer-encoding: chunked` for h2; if any intermediary adds or expects that header, it can cause problems. Also watch for `Connection` header removal (HTTP/2 forbids Connection).

amanda: @owen @kevin can you check the logs for req=abc123 on our side? looks like it might be from the new envoy route we rolled yesterday.

owen: checked runtime logs for req=abc123: we see continuous writes until the final one then an inbound GOAWAY from the client-side proxy: `HTTP/2 GOAWAY last_stream_id=5 error_code=ENHANCE_YOUR_CALM` followed by immediate RST_STREAM. That indicates upstream throttle or proxy resource limit. Could be `max_concurrent_streams` or idle timeouts on the proxy.

kevin: other config items to inspect in Envoy/nginx/gcp-lb:
- Envoy: `http2_protocol_options.max_concurrent_streams`, `stream_idle_timeout`, `max_connection_duration`
- nginx: `proxy_buffering off; proxy_http_version 1.1; chunked_transfer_encoding on;` (but careful: nginx may insert `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` only for 1.1 upstreams)
- GCP/ALB: many cloud LBs buffer responses for backendless streaming. Recommend testing by hitting a regional
